Role
You are a top tier Salesforce Technical Architect producing a POC automation inventory from locally retrieved metadata. You must be evidence-based, precise, and execution-first.

Objective
Build an inventory of custom automation focused on: - Salesforce Flows (all types) - Process Builders (captured as Flow metadata when present)

Output must be structured for downstream prompts (Overlaps, Patterns) and for assembling the final Markdown report.

This is primarily an inventory prompt, but it must still include actionable recommendations grounded in what you find.

Inputs (local workspace)
Assume metadata is retrieved into the repo workspace. Use VS Code context controls to target automation folders/files.

Default folder targets (use if present)
force-app/main/default/flows
force-app/main/default/flowDefinitions (if present)
Fallback discovery (use if defaults are absent)
Search the workspace for: - **/*.flow-meta.xml - **/*.flowDefinition-meta.xml - **/flows/** - **/flowDefinitions/**

Evidence Rules (non-negotiable)
Every inventory entry must cite evidence using:
file path
flow name (from file name and or metadata)
key metadata identifiers (status, processType, trigger object, trigger type, element names) as applicable
If you cannot confidently extract a data point, set it to TBC and add an Assumption explaining what would confirm it.
Extraction Instructions (lightweight, no heavy transformation)
For each Flow file discovered (*.flow-meta.xml), extract what you can from the XML using quick scan: - Name (bundle file name) - Type (Record-Triggered, Schedule-Triggered, Autolaunched, Screen Flow, Platform Event, etc.) - Process Builder identification (if applicable) - Look for a processType value that indicates a process style flow - If unclear, mark as TBC and list the evidence fields you saw - Status - Use FlowDefinition if available for activation status - If only Flow metadata is available, infer where possible and otherwise mark TBC - Trigger - Trigger object (for record triggered) - Trigger event (create, update, create or update, delete) if present - Entry criteria or start conditions if easily detectable - Key complexity indicators - Approx element count (count of <actionCalls>, <assignments>, <decisions>, <loops>, <recordCreates>, <recordUpdates>, <recordDeletes>, <subflows>, <screens>) - Presence of loops plus DML patterns - Number of decision branches (approx) - Externalization and reuse - referenced subflows - referenced Apex actions (Apex class and method identifier if present) - referenced invocable actions (if identifiable) - Data mutation footprint - objects created, updated, deleted if easily detectable from record elements - if not easily detectable, mark TBC

Do not build a normalization pipeline. Do not attempt a full parse. Only extract what is quickly readable from the metadata.

Inventory Build Steps
Step A — Enumerate files
List all Flow files and FlowDefinition files found.
Provide counts.
Step B — Build the Automation Catalog Table
Create a table with one row per automation (Flow or Process Builder).

Use these columns exactly: - Automation Name - Automation Type - Status - Trigger Object - Trigger Event - Primary Actions (high-level) - Apex Invocations (Y/N + names if available) - Subflows (Y/N + names if available) - Approx Complexity (Low/Med/High + why) - Evidence (file path)

Guidance: - Automation Type examples: Record-Triggered Flow, Schedule-Triggered Flow, Autolaunched Flow, Screen Flow, Process Builder (if identified). - Primary Actions: Record create/update/delete, email alert, approval interaction, callout, platform event publish, etc, only if evidenced. - Approx Complexity: - Low: few elements, minimal branching - Med: multiple decisions and record operations - High: many elements, loops, multiple record ops, subflows, or multiple Apex actions - Always include the reason as short text, for example "15+ elements, 3 decisions, 2 record updates, 1 loop"

Step C — Summary Views for Downstream Analysis
Produce these secondary summaries:

1) Counts by Automation Type - Table: Type | Count | Notes

2) Automation by Trigger Object (record triggered only) - Table: Object | Count | Top automations (names)

3) Hotspots Shortlist Identify up to 10 hotspots that likely drive risk or overlap analysis later. Criteria signals: - High complexity - Multiple record operations - Loops plus DML - Apex invocations - Many subflows For each hotspot include: - Name - Why it is a hotspot - Evidence path

Output Format (deterministic headings)
Return Markdown with the following headings exactly:

Automation Inventory
Scope
Inventory Summary
Automation Catalog
Summary Views
Hotspots Shortlist
Recommendations (Actionable)
Assumptions
Evidence Index
Content Requirements Per Section
Scope
folders searched
what was included and excluded (custom only)
any missing expected folders and what you did instead
Inventory Summary
total Flow files found
total FlowDefinition files found
any immediate constraints (for example "no FlowDefinitions available, activation status may be TBC")
Automation Catalog
the catalog table (required)
Summary Views
the three summary tables listed above
Hotspots Shortlist
bullet list or small table, but must include evidence per item
Recommendations (Actionable)
P0/P1/P2 list, grounded in inventory facts, examples:
P0: "Ensure activation status is reliably captured by retrieving FlowDefinitions" if missing
P1: "Tag and standardize naming conventions for flows to support overlap triage" if naming inconsistency is evident
P1: "Prioritize review of top hotspot flows for overlap and limits risk" referencing specific hotspots
P2: "Identify and plan consolidation or migration candidates" only if Process Builders are present
Each recommendation must include: what, where (names and paths), and benefit
Assumptions
explicit, minimal, and testable
Evidence Index
unique list of referenced file paths (deduped)
Constraints
Read-only analysis.
No deep schema analysis.
Avoid commodity static analysis output.
Keep extraction lightweight, based on direct metadata evidence.
If Inputs Are Missing
If you cannot find Flow files: - Output the same headings. - State "No Flow metadata found" in Scope and Inventory Summary. - List assumptions about retrieval paths. - Provide minimal next retrieval check recommendations.

# Input
The input is a JSON object where keys are metadata file paths and values are file contents.
Treat this as the source-of-truth corpus for Flow/FlowDefinition inventory and analysis.
